@section disagg_role_management_changes Changing a node's role

The application changes a node's role using WT_CONNECTION::reconfigure with
<code>disaggregated=(role=...)</code>. There are two role changes: <b>step-up</b> changes a follower
into a leader, and <b>step-down</b> changes a leader into a follower. Reconfiguration performs
the local storage-engine transition, not the election or replication of changes to another node.
The application must prevent competing shared writers and satisfy the transaction and checkpoint
requirements of each role change.

@section disagg_role_management_handoff Handing off shared writes

Moving shared-write responsibility from one node to another combines a step-down on the old
leader with a step-up on the new one. The last shared checkpoint the old leader takes before
stepping down is the <b>concluding checkpoint</b>; it is the base from which the new leader
continues. A complete handoff usually proceeds as follows:

1. The old leader takes the concluding checkpoint and steps down, as described in
   @ref disagg_role_management_step_down.
2. The application passes the concluding checkpoint's metadata to the node that will become the
   new leader, which adopts it through WT_CONNECTION::reconfigure with
   <code>disaggregated=(checkpoint_meta=...)</code>.
3. The new leader steps up, as described in @ref disagg_role_management_step_up. The old leader
   can write to shared storage until its step-down reconfiguration completes, so the new leader
   must not step up before then.
4. Data writes and schema operations not included in the concluding checkpoint are applied on
   the new leader, either before step-up as replicated follower writes or after it as leader
   writes. Retaining them locally on the old leader does not transfer them to another node.

Step 4 applies whether or not schema epochs are used: schema epochs select which schema
operations a checkpoint includes, but do not replace replication. The application must make the
corresponding schema available before applying data writes that depend on it.

@section disagg_role_management_step_up Step-up

Step-up is initiated by calling WT_CONNECTION::reconfigure with
<code>disaggregated=(role=leader)</code> on a follower. After the call completes, the node can
accept leader writes and produce shared checkpoints for the disaggregated database.

@subsection disagg_role_management_step_up_requirements Requirements

Transactions and positioned cursors must not span step-up. The application must finish
transactions and reset or close positioned cursors before the step-up reconfiguration.

@subsection disagg_role_management_step_up_flow Usual flow

The usual flow of step-up is:

1. Finish transactions and reset or close positioned cursors.
2. Ensure the follower has adopted the concluding checkpoint and has applied the replicated
   operations needed for it to become the new leader.
3. Call WT_CONNECTION::reconfigure with <code>disaggregated=(role=leader)</code>.
4. Start new leader operations after reconfiguration completes.

@section disagg_role_management_step_down Step-down

Step-down changes a leader into a follower by calling WT_CONNECTION::reconfigure with
<code>disaggregated=(role=follower)</code>. The node stops persisting shared writes and instead
stores replicated changes locally while continuing to read shared checkpointed data.

Every change the node accepted as leader must end up either in the concluding checkpoint or in a
local copy that survives the reconfiguration. The application chooses between two ways of
drawing that boundary: blocking step-down stops writes while the concluding checkpoint is
taken, while asynchronous step-down declares the boundary in advance so writes can continue
during the checkpoint.

@subsection disagg_role_management_blocking Blocking step-down

The simplest approach, once the application decides to step down, is to take a concluding
checkpoint and then call WT_CONNECTION::reconfigure with
<code>disaggregated=(role=follower)</code>. This approach cannot accept writes that will not be
included in the concluding checkpoint, so the application must stop serving writes while taking
that checkpoint and reconfiguring the connection.

@subsubsection disagg_role_management_blocking_requirements Requirements

- All write transactions must finish before taking the concluding checkpoint. No writes are
  allowed from the start of that checkpoint through the step-down reconfiguration.
- The stable timestamp must cover every committed change before the concluding checkpoint is
  taken.
- Cursors used for writes must be reset or closed before the step-down reconfiguration.
  Committing a transaction does not reset its cursors, and neither does an auto-commit write.

@subsubsection disagg_role_management_blocking_flow Usual flow

1. Stop new writes and finish all write transactions.
2. Advance the stable timestamp to cover their changes and take the concluding checkpoint with
   WT_SESSION::checkpoint.
3. Hand off the concluding checkpoint, reset or close cursors used for writes, and call
   WT_CONNECTION::reconfigure with <code>disaggregated=(role=follower)</code>, keeping writes
   stopped through the call.
4. Resume follower operations after reconfiguration completes.

@subsection disagg_role_management_async Asynchronous step-down

Asynchronous step-down is initiated by calling WT_CONNECTION::set_timestamp with
<code>step_down_timestamp=N</code> on a leader. N defines the concluding checkpoint's boundary:
changes committed at or below N are included, while later writes are retained locally. Reads,
writes, and schema operations can continue while the checkpoint is taken and handed to the next
leader. Writes must still stop for the step-down reconfiguration itself, so asynchronous
step-down shortens the period of write unavailability compared with blocking step-down, but does
not eliminate it.

@subsubsection disagg_role_management_async_requirements Requirements

The application must meet the following requirements and the timestamp API contract described
in WT_CONNECTION::set_timestamp:

- The step-down timestamp can only be set on a leader, and only once per step-down. Once set,
  it cannot be changed or cleared; only the step-down reconfiguration clears it, so a step-down
  cannot be abandoned after N is set.
- N must be newer than every committed transaction's commit timestamp.
- Write transactions beginning after N is set must commit with a timestamp higher than N.
- Operations on layered tables must use explicit snapshot-isolation transactions. Auto-commit,
  read-committed, and read-uncommitted operations must not occur while N is set.
- WT_SESSION::reset_snapshot must not be called while N is set.
- Prepared transactions and truncate operations must finish before N is set and must not occur
  while it is set.
- The stable timestamp must not advance above N. Both the stable timestamp and the timestamp of
  the last completed shared checkpoint must equal N at the step-down reconfiguration.
- All write transactions must finish, and cursors used for writes must be reset or closed,
  before the step-down reconfiguration. No writes or active write transactions may run during
  that call.
- Operations above N must be applied on the new leader, as described in
  @ref disagg_role_management_handoff.
- When schema epochs are used, the requirements in @ref disagg_role_management_schema also
  apply.

@subsubsection disagg_role_management_async_flow Usual flow

The usual flow of asynchronous step-down is:

1. <b>Set the step-down timestamp:</b> choose N newer than every allocated commit timestamp and
   call WT_CONNECTION::set_timestamp.
2. <b>Create the concluding checkpoint:</b> advance <code>stable_timestamp</code> to N once every
   commit at or below N has completed, and call WT_SESSION::checkpoint. WiredTiger rolls back
   write transactions that began before N was set, so the only such commits are those already
   in progress when N was set.
3. <b>Hand off the concluding checkpoint:</b> writes can continue while the next leader adopts
   it.
4. <b>Stop serving write operations:</b> stop new writes, wait for all ongoing write transactions
   to finish, and reset or close cursors used for writes. Writes must remain stopped during the
   step-down reconfiguration.
5. <b>Reconfigure:</b> call WT_CONNECTION::reconfigure with
   <code>disaggregated=(role=follower)</code> to switch WiredTiger to follower operations.
6. Resume follower operations after reconfiguration completes.

@subsubsection disagg_role_management_rollbacks Rollbacks during step-down

Some transactions that overlap an asynchronous step-down receive WT_ROLLBACK:

- A write transaction that began before N was set receives WT_ROLLBACK if it straddles setting the
step-down timestamp.
- A transaction without a read timestamp whose snapshot spans the step-down reconfiguration
  receives WT_ROLLBACK. Transactions with a read timestamp continue across the reconfiguration,
  and positioned read cursors remain usable.

When the application receives WT_ROLLBACK, it must roll back the transaction and retry the
operation in a new transaction. See @ref error_handling for rollback handling.

@subsection disagg_role_management_schema Schema operations during step-down

Data timestamps and <b>schema epochs</b> describe different boundaries: one selects record
versions, while the other selects schema operations included in shared metadata. Creating or
dropping a table locally does not publish the change to other nodes. The application
replicates schema operations, and shared checkpoints contain the corresponding metadata. With
schema epochs, WT_SESSION::publish assigns epochs to pending schema operations for a table;
checkpointing includes published operations through the stable schema epoch.

Without schema epochs, the concluding checkpoint includes the schema operations issued before it
starts, with one exception: tables created after the step-down timestamp was set are not
included.

When schema epochs are in use with asynchronous step-down,
<code>step_down_disaggregated_schema_epoch=E</code> must be provided together with the
step-down timestamp N. Before taking the concluding checkpoint, the schema operations required
through epoch E must be completed and published, and <code>stable_disaggregated_schema_epoch</code>
must advance to E. E must not be older than the existing stable schema epoch. The stable schema
epoch must not advance above E during the step-down process and must remain at E through the
step-down reconfiguration.

With blocking step-down, no step-down epoch is set. The concluding checkpoint includes published
operations through the stable schema epoch, so the application advances that epoch to cover the
schema operations being handed off before taking the checkpoint.

*/
